This chapter describes how the library constructs the modular operator and the modular
conjugation of a von Neumann algebra with a cyclic and separating vector, and how it proves
Tomita's theorem and Takesaki's KMS theorem. It corresponds to
[Section 10.10](https://hilbert6.org/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#sctn:tomita-takesaki)
of the blueprint. The section is pure operator algebra and involves no net. It builds on the
{ref "unbounded"}[chapter on unbounded operators], which supplies closures, adjoints, the spectral
measure and the functional calculus of a self-adjoint operator, and on the
{ref "stone"}[chapter on Stone's theorem], which supplies the unitary group $`e^{itA}`.

# Overview
%%%
tag := "tomita-takesaki-overview"
%%%

Let $`\mathcal{M}` be a von Neumann algebra on a Hilbert space $`H` and $`\Omega` a vector that is
cyclic and separating for $`\mathcal{M}`: the vectors $`a\Omega`, $`a \in \mathcal{M}`, are dense,
and $`a\Omega = 0` forces $`a = 0`. The map $`S_0 : a\Omega \mapsto a^*\Omega` on
$`\mathcal{M}\Omega` is then well defined, antilinear, densely defined and closable. The polar
decomposition of its closure $`S`, the _Tomita operator_,

$$`S = J\Delta^{1/2},`

produces two objects. The _modular operator_ $`\Delta = S^*S` is positive, self-adjoint and
injective; the _modular conjugation_ $`J` is an antiunitary involution with
$`J\Delta J = \Delta^{-1}`. Tomita's theorem says that they act on $`\mathcal{M}` in two ways:

$$`J\mathcal{M}J = \mathcal{M}', \qquad \Delta^{it}\mathcal{M}\Delta^{-it} = \mathcal{M} \quad (t \in \mathbb{R}).`

So $`J` exchanges $`\mathcal{M}` with its commutant, and $`\sigma_t(x) = \Delta^{it}x\Delta^{-it}`
is a one-parameter group of automorphisms of $`\mathcal{M}`, the _modular flow_, determined by the
pair $`(\mathcal{M}, \Omega)` alone.

In algebraic quantum field theory the pair comes from the vacuum. The
{ref "reeh-schlieder"}[Reeh–Schlieder theorem] shows that the vacuum is cyclic and separating for
the local von Neumann algebra of every region with spacelike room around it, so every such
algebra has a modular operator and a modular conjugation. Two results built on this are why
modular theory is central to the subject:

* _The KMS property._ The vector state $`\omega(x) = \langle \Omega, x\Omega \rangle` satisfies the
  KMS condition at inverse temperature $`1` for the reversed modular flow $`\sigma_{-t}`. Every
  faithful normal state is thus an equilibrium state for a dynamics that it determines itself.
* _The Bisognano–Wichmann theorem._ For the algebra of a wedge in a theory generated by Wightman
  fields, $`\Delta^{it}` is the unitary of a Lorentz boost of rapidity $`-2\pi t` that preserves
  the wedge, and $`J` is the CPT operator composed with a rotation by $`\pi`. The modular objects,
  defined purely algebraically, are then geometric, and the vacuum restricted to a wedge is thermal
  for the boost dynamics, the Unruh effect.

The library proves the first, Takesaki's theorem, as the last result of the section
({ref "tomita-takesaki-kms"}[below]). The library has no Wightman fields and does not prove the
Bisognano–Wichmann theorem in this form; for Haag–Kastler nets, where it is false in general, the
{ref "borchers-wedges"}[next chapter] proves Borchers' theorem, its corollary for the wedge
modular group, and the Bisognano–Wichmann theorem up to an internal symmetry under modular
covariance. What the library proves is everything of Section 10.10, with no `sorry`: the construction of $`S`, $`\Delta` and
$`J`, their basic properties, Takesaki's lemma, the description $`S^* = \overline{F_0}` of the
adjoint of the Tomita operator, the modular objects of the commutant, a strip formula for bounded
holomorphic functions, the uniqueness of Fourier transforms, Tomita's theorem,
`tomita_theorem`, and Takesaki's theorem, `isKMSState_modularAutomorphism`. The modular theory is in the namespace `Hilbert6.Modular`, the general
results on unbounded operators it needs in `Hilbert6.Spectral.Unbounded`, and the strip formula
in `Hilbert6`.

# Design
%%%
tag := "tomita-takesaki-design"
%%%

## A von Neumann algebra is a set of operators

A von Neumann algebra is a set `M : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)` satisfying `IsVonNeumannSet M`: it is closed
under the adjoint and equal to its bicommutant. The commutant is Mathlib's `Set.centralizer`, as
for the {ref "haag-kastler-main"}[local von Neumann algebras], so the statements of this chapter
apply to those algebras without translation. The algebraic structure follows from the definition:
`isVonNeumannSet_centralizer` shows that $`\mathcal{M}` and $`\mathcal{M}'` are carriers of unital
star subalgebras and that $`\mathcal{M}'` is again a von Neumann algebra.

A vector is cyclic and separating, `IsCyclicSeparatingVector M Ω`, when it is cyclic for
$`\mathcal{M}` and for $`\mathcal{M}'`, each stated as density of an orbit, the form in which the Reeh–Schlieder
theorem states cyclicity. That $`\Omega` is then separating for both algebras is the lemma
`isSeparatingVector_of_isCyclicSeparatingVector`. Since $`\mathcal{M}'' = \mathcal{M}`, the
definition is symmetric: the pair $`(\mathcal{M}', \Omega)` satisfies it as soon as
$`(\mathcal{M}, \Omega)` does.

## The antilinear Tomita operator through a reference conjugation

The Tomita operator is antilinear, while the theory of unbounded operators of the library, which
is Mathlib's `LinearPMap`, is linear: Mathlib's partial maps are semilinear in their type, but
their graph, closure, closability and adjoint exist only for linear ones. The library therefore
fixes an antiunitary involution $`C`, a _reference conjugation_, and works with the linear
operator

$$`S_0' = C \circ S_0, \qquad S_0'(a\Omega) = C(a^*\Omega).`

As a composite of two antilinear maps, $`S_0'` is linear. It is closable because its adjoint is
densely defined; its closure is $`S'`, and the Tomita operator is $`S = C \circ S'`, a function on
the domain of $`S'`. The modular operator is $`\Delta = (S')^*S'`, and the polar decomposition
$`S' = V|S'|` gives the modular conjugation $`J = CV`.

The reference conjugation is an implementation device and does not appear in the results.
`tomitaOperator_graph_eq_closure` shows that the graph of $`S` is the closure of the graph of
$`S_0`, which does not mention $`C`; the modular operator is characterised by $`S` alone, and $`J`
is the only continuous map with $`J\Delta^{1/2} = S`; so `modular_objects_independent_of_conjugation`
states that $`\Delta`, $`\Delta^{1/2}` and $`J` are the same for any two choices of $`C`. Every
Hilbert space has a reference conjugation: `referenceConjugation b` conjugates the coordinates in a
Hilbert basis `b`. The definitions take $`C` as an argument together with the involution
hypothesis `hC : ∀ x, C (C x) = x`, and the definitions that need a closed, densely defined
$`S'` also take the hypotheses `hM` and `hΩ`.

## The modular operator comes from von Neumann's theorem and polar decomposition

The library proves, for a closed, densely defined linear operator $`T`, von Neumann's theorem that
$`T^*T` is self-adjoint and positive, the absolute value $`|T| = (T^*T)^{1/2}` with
$`\operatorname{Dom}|T| = \operatorname{Dom} T` and $`\||T|\psi\| = \|T\psi\|`, and, for $`T`
injective with dense range, the polar decomposition $`T = V|T|` with a unique unitary $`V`. The
product $`T^*T` is `adjointMulSelf T`, an instance of `pmapMul B A`, whose domain is
$`\{x \in \operatorname{Dom} A \mid Ax \in \operatorname{Dom} B\}`: Mathlib's composition of
partial maps requires the range of the first factor to lie in the domain of the second, so the
product first restricts $`A`. Applied to $`T = S'`, which is closed, densely defined, injective and
has dense range because $`S` is an involution, these give $`\Delta`, $`\Delta^{1/2} = |S'|`, and
$`J`.

## Complex powers through bounded functions of the modular operator

The modular unitary group is $`\Delta^{it} = e^{it\log\Delta}`: `modularUnitaryGroup` applies the
unitary group `expUnitary` of the Stone chapter to the self-adjoint operator $`\log\Delta`, the
functional calculus of the real function $`\log`. The modular operator is injective, so its
spectral measure gives no mass to $`0` and $`\log` is harmless there.

Van Daele's proof needs $`\Delta^z` for complex $`z`, as an entire function of $`z`. On all of
$`H` this is in general an unbounded operator for $`z` off the imaginary axis. The library avoids it:
it works on the vectors $`\xi` with $`E_n\xi = \xi` for some $`n`, where $`E_n` is the spectral
projection of $`\Delta` for $`[1/n, n]`. On them $`\Delta^z` is the bounded operator
$`p_{z,n}(\Delta)`, with $`p_{z,n}(\lambda) = 1_{[1/n,n]}(\lambda)\lambda^z`, and
$`z \mapsto p_{z,n}(\Delta)\xi` is entire. These vectors are dense
({apiDocs Hilbert6.Modular.dense_modularSpectralSubspace}[`dense_modularSpectralSubspace`]),
and the only unbounded functions of $`\Delta` the proof uses are $`\Delta^{\pm 1/2}`.

## Van Daele's proof, with bounded operators

The proof of Tomita's theorem is A. van Daele's (_J. Funct. Anal._ 15 (1974) 378–393), in the
presentation of Chapter 13 of V. F. R. Jones's lecture notes _von Neumann algebras_. Its two
operator-theoretic inputs are proved with bounded operators only:

* _Takesaki's lemma_ writes $`(z - \Delta)^{-1}a'\Omega` as $`a\Omega` with $`a \in \mathcal{M}`
  for $`a' \in \mathcal{M}'` and $`z \notin [0, \infty)`. The proof follows the Hahn–Banach
  argument of M. A. Rieffel and A. van Daele (_Pacific J. Math._ 69 (1977) 187–221, Lemma 4.3),
  with the separation carried out in $`H` instead of in the predual of $`\mathcal{M}`, and an
  approximate polar decomposition $`h(\varepsilon + h^*h)^{-1/2}`, from the continuous functional
  calculus, in place of the polar decomposition of $`h`. The last step is the _commutant extension
  lemma_, `commutantExtension`: a vector $`\psi` with $`\|b'\psi\| \le c\|b'\Omega\|` for all
  $`b' \in \mathcal{M}'` is $`a\Omega` for a unique $`a \in \mathcal{M}` with $`\|a\| \le c`.
  Jones proves the corresponding step, his Lemma 13.1.5, with a Radon–Nikodym theorem of Sakai's
  type; the library derives it from Takesaki's lemma.
* _The identity_ $`S^* = \overline{F_0}`, where $`F_0 : b'\Omega \mapsto b'^*\Omega` on
  $`\mathcal{M}'\Omega`. Jones cuts off the closed operator $`c` affiliated with $`\mathcal{M}'`
  with the partial isometry of its polar decomposition, which need not be unitary. The library
  uses instead the bounded operators $`c(\mathbf{1} + |c|/n)^{-1}`: they commute with the unitaries
  of $`\mathcal{M}`, and a von Neumann algebra is spanned by its unitaries, so they lie in
  $`\mathcal{M}'`.

J. Sorce's shorter proof (_J. Funct. Anal._ (2024), arXiv:2309.16762) is not the route
formalised. Its Proposition 3.1 evaluates a contour integral of $`z^nf_k(z)(z - \Delta)^{-1}` as
if the function $`f_k(z) = 1/(1 + e^{k(z - \lambda)})` were entire, but $`f_k` has poles inside the
contour, and the norm bound of its Theorem 3.3 rests on that formula.

The library's inner product, Mathlib's, is conjugate-linear in the first argument, while Jones's
is linear in the first, so Jones's $`\langle u, v \rangle` appears in the library as
$`\langle v, u \rangle` and his formulas are transposed accordingly.

